00:00:33.678 --> 00:00:40.570
The  word that comes to our minds when bacteria
take the stage is “chaos.”

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:45.580
Each individual may be so small as to feel
insignificant, but the combined presence of

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:51.829
all of those tiny shapes in so many different
states of excitement can be difficult to make

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:52.829
sense of.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:59.150
In fact, in the 18th century, Carl von Linné
grouped the microbes he observed together

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.450
and named them “Chaos.”

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:07.030
The label didn’t last in part because it
wasn’t exactly specific, but the feeling

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.040
endures.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.010
Even when the movement seems more ordered,
it’s baffling to watch.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:20.590
Like these little wiggles that seem so snake-like
except for the unsettling vibration that’s

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:21.590
added on.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:27.650
Or you might see a mass of bacteria collectively
spinning in place like a very odd, microscopic

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.280
flash mob.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.100
Or you could throw them all together on the
same slide and end up with what looks like

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.760
several different dance parties at once.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.830
But while the movements may be a bit much
to take in when witnessed all at once, the

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:46.770
source of that movement is almost always the
product of the same masterpiece of bacterial

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.909
ingenuity: the bacterial flagella.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:54.490
We’ve been wanting to talk about bacterial
flagella for a while, but it’s been tough

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.840
because well they’re just small and they’re
difficult to make out in our footage.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:04.770
Fortunately, with a lot of patience and magnification,
we have finally been able to spot them for

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.539
the first time in our microcosmos journey.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:13.400
But we have to zoom in a bit more on this
clip, and you will need to ignore the rounder

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.530
protists buzzing around.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:22.450
Instead, focus on the rod-shaped bacteria,
and you will see these faint lines coming

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.370
off of them.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.440
Those are the flagella.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.840
More specifically, each strand you see is
a filament, which is anchored to a joint called

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.819
the hook, which then connects to a motor enclosed
in the bacteria itself.

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:43.370
Words like “hook” and “motor” imply
something made from human hands, with metal

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.819
bits of machinery and fuel.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:52.650
But biology builds with its own materials,
and the flagella is a complex assembly made

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.430
out of proteins that move like gears.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:02.819
And it does function very much like one of
our motors, with a stator and a rotor and

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:04.819
gears that connect between them.

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:09.910
But, of course, both arose independently because
if you want something to rotate, there is

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:16.280
a best way to do it, and both nature and humans,
in this case, came up with the same solution.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.420
Though, of course for bacteria the movement
of the motor is powered by an electrochemical

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:27.330
potential created by pumping ions across the
cell membrane instead of by an internal combustion

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:28.330
engine.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.580
Now if you’ve been on this journey with
us for a while, you might be wondering, “Why

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.410
are you talking about flagella like it’s
a new thing?”

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:40.180
We’ve talked about tons of flagellates before,
and even explained how they move.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.490
But there is a difference here.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.690
Those flagellates are eukaryotes.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:52.299
And while the name is the same, the actual
appendage is definitely not.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:59.030
The bacterial flagella is a hollow tube that
sticks out of the cell and rotates as dictated

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.099
by its motor.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:02.780
It actually spins around.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:09.090
The eukaryotic flagella is a bundle of microtubules
surrounded by cell membrane and it does not

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:14.180
rotate, it just flips back and forth to whip
around to move the cell.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.999
And unlike the ion gradients used to power
bacterial flagella, eukaryotes rely on the

00:04:18.999 --> 00:04:22.729
energy-storing molecule ATP to fuel theirs.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:27.569
So, why do these two different assemblies
have the same name?

00:04:27.569 --> 00:04:32.581
Well, probably simply because when we were
originally looking at them, we didn’t know

00:04:32.581 --> 00:04:34.139
any of this.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:41.009
Understanding the biochemistry of these marvelous
machines has been a very long, involved, careful

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:46.020
process involving thousands of scientists
over decades.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:51.339
One of the original tests to see how different
bacterial flagella were from eukaryotic flagella

00:04:51.339 --> 00:04:57.319
actually involved basically taping the bacterial
flagella down.

00:04:57.319 --> 00:05:03.249
And they found that when they did that, instead
of flopping back and forth, the bacteria themselves

00:05:03.249 --> 00:05:05.689
spun around and around.

00:05:05.689 --> 00:05:11.389
The movement of bacteria stood out to early
observers for the same reason it stands out

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:14.020
to us: because it is so weird.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.860
There’s a mix of sporadic movements and
directed movements.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:24.849
But even with the significant lack of grace,
there is something coordinated underlying

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:26.250
this movement.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:32.039
It comes down to what direction the bacteria’s
flagella are rotating.

00:05:32.039 --> 00:05:38.279
When the motor turns the flagella clockwise,
the filaments form a supercoil that bundles

00:05:38.279 --> 00:05:42.569
together and that drives the bacteria straight.

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:49.259
But when the flagella is turned counter-clockwise,
the bundle comes undone, and each individual

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:51.539
flagella moves on its own.

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:59.099
And that creates disarray, causing the bacteria
to tumble randomly around the microcosmos.

00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:04.259
In our world, stumbling around randomly might
seem like a bit of a liability.

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:10.740
But for bacteria, this is an evolutionarily
planned affair, helping the bacteria find

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.689
food and light.

00:06:12.689 --> 00:06:18.090
Tumbling around lets the bacteria sample the
world around them, looking for chemical scents

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:23.520
or hints of light that might point them towards
the resources they need to survive.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.410
And when they catch those signals, whether
it’s a ray of light or a chemical gradient,

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:34.919
the flagella turn clockwise to set the bacteria
on a straight course towards their next home.

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:41.520
So while at first glance it seems messy and
even a little broken, the bacterial flagella,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:50.439
it turns out, is one of the most majestic,
useful, and powerful structures in biology.

00:06:50.439 --> 00:06:59.110
Barely visible, but in our opinion, completely
deserving of a fan club all its own.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:04.129
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:05.589
us.

